-Also called: Sparse Voxel Grid Optimization, Coarse-to-Fine Optimization, PlenOctree Optimization
+Also called: Explicit Radiance Fields Optimization, Plenoxels Training, Coarse-to-Fine Voxel Optimization
 
 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Grid Optimization refers to a family of training strategies for 3D radiance fields that utilize structured, multi-resolution data structures (such as sparse voxel grids or octrees) rather than dense neural networks or unstructured point clouds. It solves the memory and computational bottlenecks associated with high-resolution scene representations by dynamically adapting the grid density to the scene's complexity [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) by replacing MLPs with explicit grid lookups, and from [3D Gaussian Splatting](1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) by using axis-aligned, spatially disjoint voxels with explicit continuity enforcement [S2, unofficial].
+Hierarchical Grid Optimization (HGO) refers to a family of methods that optimize explicit 3D grid representations (voxels, octrees) for radiance fields, avoiding the computational overhead of neural network inference during training. It solves the memory and speed bottlenecks of implicit neural representations by directly optimizing density and color parameters stored in spatial data structures [S1]. Unlike [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) which use MLPs, HGO methods leverage explicit data access for real-time rendering and faster convergence [S1]. The family encompasses diverse strategies: non-linear least-squares solvers for explicit fields [S1], fixed-grid optimization with spherical harmonics [S1], and dynamic coarse-to-fine refinement with pruning [S1].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family rely on the differentiability of the forward rendering operator and the propagation of gradients through the integration steps. The core mechanism is the evaluation of the volume rendering equation and the computation of gradients with respect to scene parameters (density, geometry, transfer functions, camera poses).
+All methods in this family optimize explicit 3D structures (grids or trees) to minimize a photometric loss between rendered and ground-truth images. The core mechanism relies on the differentiability of the volume rendering equation with respect to voxel densities ($\sigma$) and color coefficients [S1, S2].
 
